    return html`&lt;div&gt;
      &lt;div style="font-weight:600;color:${s.color};margin-bottom:.2rem"&gt;&lt;code&gt;${s.key}()&lt;/code&gt;: ${s.label}&lt;/div&gt;
      ${coveragePlot(reps, {w: pw, unit: "°C", ylim: 3})}
      &lt;div class="ac-readouts"&gt;
        &lt;div&gt;&lt;div class="ac-label"&gt;CIs that caught the truth&lt;/div&gt;
          &lt;div class="readout" style="color:${p.cover &gt;= 0.9 ? col.blue : col.red}"&gt;${Math.round(100 * p.cover)}%&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
        &lt;div&gt;&lt;div class="ac-label"&gt;Median CI width&lt;/div&gt;
          &lt;div class="readout"&gt;${p.width.toFixed(2)} °C&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;`;
  });

  return html`&lt;div class="widget"&gt;
    ${svg.node()}
    &lt;div class="ac-note"&gt;Each point summarizes 200 simulated summers of 60 daily readings. The gray band marks
      the ρ chosen above, shown in detail below. (Bar colors below: blue caught the truth, red missed.)&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div style="display:${wide ? "grid" : "block"};grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:24px;margin-top:1rem"&gt;
      ${panels}
    &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;`;
}








With no autocorrelation, the two models agree: same widths, about 95% coverage. gls() costs little when it isn’t needed
As \(\rho\) grows, lm() keeps reporting a narrow CI (it even shrinks a little) while its coverage collapses
gls() widens its CIs to match and stays much closer to 95%
Not perfect: with 60 readings, gls() must estimate \(\rho\) too, so it falls a bit short at strong correlation (and now and then gives a huge CI when it can’t tell the mean from a slow drift). Good sampling design still beats any model fix
